Carafe: the effect thread and the joined passes also work for games that load D3DX as a DLL. When the executable has
no effect library of its own, the hooks look in the loaded d3dx9_24 to d3dx9_43, which are Wine's D3DX in Carafe's
runtime: their effect interface is the one table of 77 to 79 code pointers whose parameter setters, SetTechnique,
Begin, BeginPass, CommitChanges, EndPass and End each pop the argument bytes their signatures have. Wine's interface
has these methods two slots before the effect library linked into older games, so the front end takes the slots of
the layout found instead of fixed ones. Wine's D3DX touches no device state in EndPass, does nothing in CommitChanges
outside a pass and commits the parameters changed since a pass was applied, as the joined passes need.
Zlib, as the files it changes.

